::ALG1_U03_L01_GP_Q01::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U03_L01_GP_Q01<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A1.LR.1<br><br>Question:<br>A relationship has points (0, 2), (1, 5), (2, 8), (3, 11). What is the rate of change? {
~A. 2 #This is the starting output, not the amount the output changes each step.
=B. 3 #Correct. The output increases by 3 each time the input increases by 1.
~C. 5 #This is one output value, not the repeated change.
~D. 11 #This is the last output shown, not the rate of change.
}

::ALG1_U03_L01_GP_Q02::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U03_L01_GP_Q02<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A1.LR.1<br><br>Question:<br>Inputs are 0, 1, 2, 3 and outputs are 4, 9, 14, 19. Is the relationship linear? {
~A. No, because the outputs are different. #Outputs can be different in a linear relationship; the changes must be constant.
~B. No, because the inputs start at 0. #Starting at 0 does not prevent a relationship from being linear.
~C. Yes, because the outputs increase by 4. #The outputs do not increase by 4; they increase by 5.
=D. Yes, because the outputs increase by 5 each time. #Correct. Equal input steps have equal output changes.
}

::ALG1_U03_L01_GP_Q03::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U03_L01_GP_Q03<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A1.LR.1<br><br>Question:<br>A parking garage charges $6 per hour. Which ordered pairs match hours x and cost y? {
=A. (1, 6), (2, 12), (3, 18) #Correct. The cost is 6 times the number of hours.
~B. (1, 7), (2, 8), (3, 9) #This adds 6 only once instead of multiplying by each hour.
~C. (6, 1), (12, 2), (18, 3) #The input and output values are reversed.
~D. (0, 6), (1, 6), (2, 6) #This shows a constant cost, not $6 per hour.
}

::ALG1_U03_L01_GP_Q04::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U03_L01_GP_Q04<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A1.LR.1<br><br>Question:<br>A plant starts at 10 inches and grows 3 inches each week. What are the starting value and rate of change? {
~A. Starting value 3, rate 10 #This reverses the initial height and weekly growth.
~B. Starting value 13, rate 3 #13 is the height after one week, not the starting value.
=C. Starting value 10, rate 3 #Correct. The plant begins at 10 and increases by 3 each week.
~D. Starting value 10, rate 13 #13 is not the repeated weekly change.
}

::ALG1_U03_L01_GP_Q05::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U03_L01_GP_Q05<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A1.LR.1<br><br>Question:<br>How can a table show that a relationship is linear? {
~A. The outputs must all be the same. #Equal outputs show no change; linear relationships can increase or decrease.
=B. Equal input changes must produce equal output changes. #Correct. Constant change is the key sign of a linear relationship in a table.
~C. The largest output must be first. #The order of outputs does not define linearity.
~D. The table must have exactly two rows. #A table can have many rows and still show a linear relationship.
}
